from (values
('112.42.c.9.A',3,'Pelvic bones in a whale are very small and have no clear use. What type of structure are they?','["homologous in function", "newly evolved", "vestigial", "analogous"]'::jsonb,2,'They are remnants from ancestors that walked on land.','original'),
('112.42.c.9.A',4,'Two species share a very high percentage of DNA. What does that suggest?','["they have the same diet", "they look alike by chance", "they live in the same habitat", "they share a recent common ancestor"]'::jsonb,3,'More similar DNA suggests closer relation.','original'),
('112.42.c.9.A',4,'Marsupials are found mostly in Australia and nearby islands. How does biogeography explain this?','["Related groups spread and diversified after the continents separated", "Marsupials cannot survive elsewhere", "Australia has no predators for them", "Marsupials were created there"]'::jsonb,0,'Geographic isolation led to unique lineages.','original'),
('112.42.c.9.B',2,'Which idea says that species change slowly and steadily over long periods?','["punctuated equilibrium", "gradualism", "extinction", "stasis"]'::jsonb,1,'Small changes accumulate over time.','original'),
('112.42.c.9.B',3,'Which idea says that long periods of little change are interrupted by short bursts of rapid change?','["genetic drift", "gradualism", "punctuated equilibrium", "inheritance of acquired traits"]'::jsonb,2,'The fossil record often shows such bursts.','original'),
('112.42.c.9.B',3,'What is stasis in the fossil record?','["a sudden extinction", "rapid change in many species", "the formation of a fossil", "a long period with little or no change in a species"]'::jsonb,3,'The species stays nearly the same.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.A',3,'Which can evolve over generations?','["a population", "a single cell during its life", "a single organism", "a single gene during an individual''s life"]'::jsonb,0,'Allele frequencies change in populations.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.A',3,'Soot darkens tree bark, and dark peppered moths become more common than light ones. What explains this?','["Light moths migrated away on their own", "Dark moths were better hidden and survived more often", "Light moths turned dark", "Dark moths were created by the soot"]'::jsonb,1,'Natural selection favors traits that help survival.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.A',3,'What does fitness mean in biology?','["how large an organism is", "how strong an organism is", "how well an organism survives and reproduces", "how fast an organism is"]'::jsonb,2,'Fitness is reproductive success.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.A',4,'Why can''t an individual organism evolve through natural selection?','["evolution only happens to cells", "natural selection acts on DNA only", "individuals never change at all", "its genes stay the same during its life, so selection changes the population"]'::jsonb,3,'Allele frequencies change across generations.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.B',3,'Which is NOT required for natural selection?','["traits acquired during an organism''s life being passed on", "variation among individuals", "traits that are inherited", "more offspring than can survive"]'::jsonb,0,'Only inherited variation is acted on.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.B',3,'Why do some bacteria become resistant to antibiotics over time?','["Antibiotics change the bacteria''s DNA on purpose", "Resistant bacteria survive treatment and reproduce more", "All bacteria are always resistant", "Bacteria choose to become resistant"]'::jsonb,1,'Selection favors the resistant variants.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.B',3,'What does it mean that populations have the potential to overproduce?','["they produce exactly the number that survive", "they never reproduce", "they make more offspring than the environment can support", "all the offspring survive"]'::jsonb,2,'That leads to competition.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.B',3,'What is an adaptation?','["a trait an organism learns", "a change in the habitat", "a mutation that always helps", "an inherited trait that improves survival and reproduction in an environment"]'::jsonb,3,'Adaptations arise through natural selection.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.C',3,'Which event can lead to the formation of a new species?','["geographic isolation of populations", "unchanging environments for all populations", "learning new behaviors only", "constant interbreeding among them"]'::jsonb,0,'Isolated populations diverge.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.C',3,'By the biological species concept, a species is a group that can do what?','["look exactly alike", "interbreed and produce fertile offspring", "eat the same food", "live in the same place"]'::jsonb,1,'Reproductive isolation defines separate species.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.C',4,'A river splits a squirrel population, and over time the two groups can no longer mate. What is this an example of?','["extinction", "gene flow", "speciation", "a single mutation"]'::jsonb,2,'Isolation and different selection lead to new species.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.C',4,'What is adaptive radiation?','["the movement of animals between populations", "the extinction of many species", "the loss of variation", "rapid diversification of one ancestral species into many forms for different niches"]'::jsonb,3,'Darwin''s finches are an example.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.D',3,'What is genetic drift?','["random changes in allele frequencies, especially in small populations", "movement of genes between populations", "changes caused by the environment selecting traits", "changes due to learning"]'::jsonb,0,'Chance events change allele frequencies.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.D',3,'What is gene flow?','["the death of a population", "the movement of alleles between populations through migration", "random change in allele frequencies", "the copying of DNA"]'::jsonb,1,'Migrants carry alleles into new populations.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.D',4,'A disease kills most of a population, leaving a few survivors with a limited set of alleles. What is this called?','["mutation", "gene flow", "a bottleneck effect", "natural selection only"]'::jsonb,2,'Genetic variation drops after a sharp population decline.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.D',3,'What is the ultimate source of new alleles in a population?','["genetic drift", "gene flow only", "natural selection", "mutation"]'::jsonb,3,'Mutations create new versions of genes.','original'),
('112.42.c.10.D',4,'A few individuals colonize a new island, and their small gene pool differs from the original. What is this called?','["the founder effect", "a bottleneck effect", "adaptive radiation", "gene flow"]'::jsonb,0,'A small starting group may not represent the whole population.','original'),
('112.42.c.11.A',2,'What are the reactants of photosynthesis?','["ATP and glucose", "carbon dioxide and water", "glucose and oxygen", "oxygen and water"]'::jsonb,1,'Light energy is also needed.','original'),
('112.42.c.11.A',2,'What are the products of photosynthesis?','["carbon dioxide and water", "ATP and carbon dioxide", "glucose and oxygen", "lipids and water"]'::jsonb,2,'Plants make sugar and release O₂.','original'),
('112.42.c.11.A',2,'Where does most cellular respiration take place in a eukaryotic cell?','["the cell wall", "ribosomes", "chloroplasts", "mitochondria"]'::jsonb,3,'Mitochondria produce most of a cell''s ATP.','original'),
('112.42.c.11.A',3,'What are the products of aerobic cellular respiration?','["carbon dioxide, water and ATP", "lactic acid and alcohol", "glucose and oxygen", "oxygen and protein"]'::jsonb,0,'Glucose is broken down with oxygen.','original'),
('112.42.c.11.A',3,'A student says that matter is lost during cellular respiration. What is the correct idea?','["atoms disappear as heat only", "atoms are rearranged into new molecules, so matter is conserved", "atoms are destroyed", "atoms turn into energy"]'::jsonb,1,'Conservation of matter holds in all chemical reactions.','original'),
('112.42.c.11.A',3,'Which pigment in chloroplasts captures light for photosynthesis?','["hemoglobin", "melanin", "chlorophyll", "keratin"]'::jsonb,2,'Chlorophyll absorbs mostly red and blue light.','original'),
('112.42.c.11.B',3,'How do enzymes speed up reactions?','["by being used up in the reaction", "by changing the products", "by adding heat to the reaction", "by lowering the activation energy"]'::jsonb,3,'Enzymes are catalysts and are not consumed.','original'),
('112.42.c.11.B',3,'Where does the substrate bind on an enzyme?','["the active site", "the nucleus", "the membrane lipid", "the cell wall"]'::jsonb,0,'Its shape fits the substrate.','original'),
('112.42.c.11.B',3,'Why does an enzyme stop working at very high temperature?','["it floats away from the cell", "its shape changes (denatures), so the active site no longer fits", "it gets bigger and stronger", "it turns into a lipid"]'::jsonb,1,'Denaturation destroys the active site shape.','original'),
('112.42.c.11.B',3,'Pepsin works best in the acidic stomach. What does this show?','["enzymes are alive", "enzymes work at any pH", "enzymes have an optimum pH", "enzymes are made of sugar"]'::jsonb,2,'Different enzymes work best at different pH values.','original')
