('112.42.c.12.A',3,'Which two systems mainly regulate the body''s activities through signals?','["skeletal and muscular", "integumentary and respiratory", "digestive and excretory", "nervous and endocrine"]'::jsonb,3,'Neurons use electrical signals and glands use hormones.','original'),
('112.42.c.12.A',3,'After nutrients are absorbed in the small intestine, which system carries them to body cells?','["circulatory", "skeletal", "integumentary", "respiratory"]'::jsonb,0,'Blood transports absorbed nutrients.','original'),
('112.42.c.12.A',3,'What is the role of villi in the small intestine?','["make bile", "increase surface area for absorption", "store waste", "grind food"]'::jsonb,1,'Folds and projections increase absorption.','original'),
('112.42.c.12.A',4,'Blood sugar rises after a meal, and the pancreas releases insulin, which lowers it. This is an example of what?','["mutation", "positive feedback", "negative feedback", "diffusion only"]'::jsonb,2,'The response reverses the change.','original'),
('112.42.c.12.B',3,'Which plant tissue carries water and minerals upward from the roots?','["epidermis", "pith only", "phloem", "xylem"]'::jsonb,3,'Xylem moves water upward.','original'),
('112.42.c.12.B',3,'Which plant tissue carries sugars made in the leaves to other parts?','["phloem", "bark only", "xylem", "root hairs"]'::jsonb,0,'Phloem transports sugar in sap.','original'),
('112.42.c.12.B',3,'What do stomata do?','["absorb water from soil", "allow gas exchange and water vapor loss", "hold the plant upright", "make seeds"]'::jsonb,1,'Guard cells open and close them.','original'),
('112.42.c.12.B',3,'Phototropism is a plant''s growth response to what?','["gravity", "touch", "light", "water"]'::jsonb,2,'Stems bend toward light.','original'),
('112.42.c.12.B',4,'Which plant hormone helps cause stems to bend toward light?','["thyroxine", "adrenaline", "insulin", "auxin"]'::jsonb,3,'Auxin builds up on the shaded side and lengthens those cells.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.A',2,'Bees collect nectar from flowers while pollinating them. This is an example of what?','["mutualism", "parasitism", "predation", "competition"]'::jsonb,0,'Both species benefit.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.A',2,'A tapeworm lives in the intestines of a dog and takes nutrients, harming it. This is an example of what?','["mutualism", "parasitism", "commensalism", "competition"]'::jsonb,1,'The parasite benefits and the host is harmed.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.A',3,'Birds build nests in a tree, which benefits the birds but does not affect the tree. This is an example of what?','["predation", "mutualism", "commensalism", "parasitism"]'::jsonb,2,'One species benefits and the other is unaffected.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.A',2,'Two species of plants compete for the same limited sunlight. This is an example of what?','["predation", "commensalism", "mutualism", "competition"]'::jsonb,3,'They use the same limited resource.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.A',3,'A lynx hunts snowshoe hares. This relationship is an example of what?','["predation", "mutualism", "parasitism", "commensalism"]'::jsonb,0,'The predator kills and eats the prey.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.B',3,'About how much energy is passed from one trophic level to the next?','["100 percent", "about 10 percent", "about 90 percent", "about 50 percent"]'::jsonb,1,'Most energy is lost as heat.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.B',3,'Which organisms form the base of most food chains?','["herbivores", "decomposers", "producers", "carnivores"]'::jsonb,2,'Producers capture energy from sunlight.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.B',4,'If the top predator in a food web is removed, what might happen to the herbivore population?','["it will definitely go extinct", "it will stay exactly the same", "it will turn into producers", "it may grow and overgraze the producers"]'::jsonb,3,'Predators limit prey populations.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.B',3,'Which statement about energy and matter in an ecosystem is true?','["energy flows in one direction while matter is recycled", "neither moves through the ecosystem", "both are completely recycled", "matter flows one way while energy is recycled"]'::jsonb,0,'Energy enters as sunlight and leaves as heat.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.B',3,'What role do decomposers play in an ecosystem?','["eat living plants", "break down dead matter and return nutrients to the soil", "make food from sunlight", "hunt other animals"]'::jsonb,1,'They recycle nutrients.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.C',3,'What do nitrogen-fixing bacteria do?','["turn plants into soil", "make carbon dioxide", "convert nitrogen gas into forms plants can use", "remove oxygen"]'::jsonb,2,'They make ammonia from atmospheric N₂.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.C',3,'Burning fossil fuels affects the carbon cycle in what way?','["it removes carbon from the air", "it makes oxygen only", "it creates nitrogen", "it releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere"]'::jsonb,3,'Stored carbon is released as CO₂.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.C',3,'What process removes carbon dioxide from the atmosphere into living things?','["photosynthesis", "combustion", "decomposition", "evaporation"]'::jsonb,0,'Plants convert CO₂ into sugars.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.C',4,'Fertilizer runoff reaches a lake and causes a large algae bloom. What problem may follow?','["colder water", "oxygen levels drop and aquatic life dies", "less algae the next year", "more oxygen for fish"]'::jsonb,1,'Decomposing algae use up oxygen.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.C',4,'Clearing large forests affects the carbon cycle how?','["oxygen increases", "more carbon dioxide is absorbed", "less carbon dioxide is absorbed by plants", "carbon dioxide is destroyed"]'::jsonb,2,'Fewer plants mean less photosynthesis.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.D',3,'What is the leading cause of biodiversity loss?','["too much rain", "too much sunlight", "too many predators", "habitat destruction"]'::jsonb,3,'Species lose the places where they live.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.D',3,'How can an invasive species harm an ecosystem?','["it can outcompete native species for resources", "it always helps native species", "it never competes", "it adds biodiversity every time"]'::jsonb,0,'Invasive species often have no natural predators.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.D',3,'Why are ecosystems with high biodiversity often more stable?','["they have fewer species", "if one species declines, others can fill its role", "they have only producers", "they never change"]'::jsonb,1,'Many species provide redundancy.','original'),
('112.42.c.13.D',4,'What is a keystone species?','["the largest species", "the smallest species", "a species with a large effect on its ecosystem relative to its numbers", "the most abundant species"]'::jsonb,2,'Removing it can change the whole community.','original')
